{
  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The protection afforded to St Ilven Hospital under Article 19 of the Fourth Geneva Convention and Article 13 of Additional Protocol I lawfully ceased before 13 August 2025 because the hospital was used to commit acts harmful to the enemy and the required warning was given and remained unheeded.",
      "reasoning": "Both Article 19 GC IV and Article 13 AP I stipulate that protection does not cease unless the medical facility is used for acts harmful to the enemy, and only after a due warning with a reasonable time‑limit has been ignored. From 2 August the hospital’s top floor housed an artillery observation team, the roof mounted a machine‑gun that fired on Calvaran troops, and the basement stored ammunition – uses that go beyond humanitarian functions and constitute acts harmful to the enemy. On 9 August Calvaran forces issued a clear warning, via radio, leaflets and an ICRC delegate, giving a 48‑hour deadline for the cessation of such use. The military use continued on 10 and 11 August, and evacuation of patients only occurred on 12 August, meaning the warning was unheeded. Accordingly, the statutory conditions for loss of protection were satisfied.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "gc_iv",
          "article": "19",
          "quoted_text": "The protection to which civilian hospitals are entitled shall not cease unless they are used to commit, outside their humanitarian duties, acts harmful to the enemy. Protection may, however, cease only after due warning has been given, naming, in all appropriate cases, a reasonable time limit, and after such warning has remained unheeded."
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "13",
          "quoted_text": "The protection to which civilian medical units are entitled shall not cease unless they are used to commit, outside their humanitarian function, acts harmful to the enemy. Protection may, however, cease only after a warning has been given setting, whenever appropriate, a reasonable time-limit, and after such warning has remained unheeded."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The warning issued on 9 August satisfied the statutory requirement of a \"reasonable time‑limit\" and therefore the continuation of military use until 12 August constituted an unheeded warning, triggering loss of protection.",
      "reasoning": "Article 19 GC IV and Article 13 AP I require that the warning set a reasonable time‑limit and that protection only ceases after the warning remains unheeded. International practice interprets a 48‑hour period, as provided in the factual record, as reasonable where the warning is communicated by multiple means (radio, leaflets, ICRC delegate). The fact that the hospital’s armed functions persisted for the full 48 hours demonstrates that the warning was not complied with, satisfying the unheeded condition.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "gc_iv",
          "article": "19",
          "quoted_text": "Protection may, however, cease only after due warning has been given, naming, in all appropriate cases, a reasonable time limit, and after such warning has remained unheeded."
